WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.140
Many Bash pipelines rely on text-processing tools such as grep, awk, sed, cut, sort, and similar utilities.

2
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:14.920
These tools are valuable, but they depend on assumptions about input format.

3
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:19.020
When those assumptions are not documented or tested, the pipeline becomes brittle.

4
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:23.220
Human-readable output is often not a stable machine interface.

5
00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:31.320
A tool may add a column, change spacing, localize labels, include headers, or alter output between versions.

6
00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:37.100
A script that depends on a visual layout may keep running while selecting the wrong field or missing important records.

7
00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.180
Delimiters and columns deserve careful review.

8
00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:43.320
A delimiter may appear inside a value.

9
00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:45.420
An empty field may be meaningful.

10
00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.120
Whitespace may be collapsed or preserved differently than expected.

11
00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:53.140
Sorting and matching may depend on locale or encoding.

12
00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:57.320
These are not edge cases when a pipeline supports production automation.

13
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.800
Where available, prefer structured output.

14
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.820
Formats designed for machines are easier to validate than free-form text.

15
00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:10.120
Even then, the script should check required fields and handle missing or unexpected data.

16
00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:15.100
Structured output reduces ambiguity, but it does not remove the need for validation.

17
00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:20.420
When human-readable output must be parsed, document the assumption and test it.

18
00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:28.760
Include no-match cases, malformed lines, empty fields, unexpected values, and representative production-like samples.

19
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:37.260
If a parsing decision can affect deployment, monitoring, reporting, or data movement, it deserves tests before production use.

20
00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:40.900
Secure filtering is not about avoiding classic shell tools.

21
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:49.000
It is about using them with clear input expectations, explicit failure handling, and an understanding that text shape is part of the contract.
